The table structure information is (table names): 
['ERD', 'movies', 'genre', 'director_mapping', 'role_mapping', 'names', 'ratings']
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• genre.movie_id: join key to match each genre entry to its film.  
• genre.genre: genre name we need to aggregate.  
• ratings.movie_id: join key to genre / director_mapping.  
• ratings.avg_rating: filter condition “avg_rating > 8”.  
• director_mapping.movie_id: join key to know which director(s) worked on each film.  
• director_mapping.name_id: director identifier we will later count.  
• names.id: join key to link a director’s id to his / her real name.  
• names.name: director’s full name to be finally displayed.

Main conditions that will be used  
1. ratings.avg_rating > 8  → keeps only “high‑rated” movies.  
2. genre IN (top‑3 genres by high‑rated count)  → keeps only the selected genres for the director part.

Below are 10 exploratory → progressively complex SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows) that let you inspect the data step by step up to the final answer.

```sql
-- 1) Inspect some movies that are rated above 8
SELECT DISTINCT "movie_id", "avg_rating"
FROM "ratings"
WHERE "avg_rating" > 8
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) See which genres those highly‑rated movies belong to
SELECT DISTINCT g."genre"
FROM "genre" AS g
JOIN "ratings"  r ON r."movie_id" = g."movie_id"
WHERE r."avg_rating" > 8
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Count how many high‑rated (>8) movies each genre has
SELECT g."genre",
       COUNT(DISTINCT g."movie_id") AS "cnt_high_rated"
FROM "genre" AS g
JOIN "ratings" r ON r."movie_id" = g."movie_id"
WHERE r."avg_rating" > 8
GROUP BY g."genre"
ORDER BY "cnt_high_rated" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Retrieve the TOP‑3 genres having the most movies rated above 8
SELECT g."genre",
       COUNT(DISTINCT g."movie_id") AS "cnt_high_rated"
FROM "genre" AS g
JOIN "ratings" r ON r."movie_id" = g."movie_id"
WHERE r."avg_rating" > 8
GROUP BY g."genre"
ORDER BY "cnt_high_rated" DESC
LIMIT 3;
```

```sql
-- 5) List movie IDs that both (a) are >8 rated and (b) belong to the TOP‑3 genres
WITH top_gen AS (
    SELECT g."genre"
    FROM "genre" g
    JOIN "ratings" r ON r."movie_id" = g."movie_id"
    WHERE r."avg_rating" > 8
    GROUP BY g."genre"
    ORDER BY COUNT(DISTINCT g."movie_id") DESC
    LIMIT 3
)
SELECT DISTINCT g."movie_id", g."genre"
FROM "genre" g
JOIN "ratings" r    ON r."movie_id" = g."movie_id"
JOIN top_gen  tg    ON tg."genre"  = g."genre"
WHERE r."avg_rating" > 8
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) For those movies, find all associated director IDs
WITH top_gen AS (
    SELECT g."genre"
    FROM "genre" g
    JOIN "ratings" r ON r."movie_id" = g."movie_id"
    WHERE r."avg_rating" > 8
    GROUP BY g."genre"
    ORDER BY COUNT(DISTINCT g."movie_id") DESC
    LIMIT 3
), top_movies AS (
    SELECT DISTINCT g."movie_id"
    FROM "genre" g
    JOIN "ratings" r ON r."movie_id" = g."movie_id"
    JOIN top_gen tg  ON tg."genre" = g."genre"
    WHERE r."avg_rating" > 8
)
SELECT DISTINCT dm."name_id"
FROM "director_mapping" dm
JOIN top_movies tm ON tm."movie_id" = dm."movie_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Count how many qualifying high‑rated movies each director made
WITH top_gen AS (
    SELECT g."genre"
    FROM "genre" g
    JOIN "ratings" r ON r."movie_id" = g."movie_id"
    WHERE r."avg_rating" > 8
    GROUP BY g."genre"
    ORDER BY COUNT(DISTINCT g."movie_id") DESC
    LIMIT 3
), top_movies AS (
    SELECT DISTINCT g."movie_id"
    FROM "genre" g
    JOIN "ratings" r ON r."movie_id" = g."movie_id"
    JOIN top_gen tg  ON tg."genre" = g."genre"
    WHERE r."avg_rating" > 8
)
SELECT dm."name_id",
       COUNT(DISTINCT dm."movie_id") AS "num_high_rated_movies"
FROM "director_mapping" dm
JOIN top_movies tm ON tm."movie_id" = dm."movie_id"
GROUP BY dm."name_id"
ORDER BY "num_high_rated_movies" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Pick the TOP‑4 directors (by count) in those genres
WITH top_gen AS (
    SELECT g."genre"
    FROM "genre" g
    JOIN "ratings" r ON r."movie_id" = g."movie_id"
    WHERE r."avg_rating" > 8
    GROUP BY g."genre"
    ORDER BY COUNT(DISTINCT g."movie_id") DESC
    LIMIT 3
), top_movies AS (
    SELECT DISTINCT g."movie_id"
    FROM "genre" g
    JOIN "ratings" r ON r."movie_id" = g."movie_id"
    JOIN top_gen tg ON tg."genre" = g."genre"
    WHERE r."avg_rating" > 8
), dir_counts AS (
    SELECT dm."name_id",
           COUNT(DISTINCT dm."movie_id") AS num_high_rated_movies
    FROM "director_mapping" dm
    JOIN top_movies tm ON tm."movie_id" = dm."movie_id"
    GROUP BY dm."name_id"
)
SELECT "name_id", "num_high_rated_movies"
FROM dir_counts
ORDER BY "num_high_rated_movies" DESC
LIMIT 4;
```

```sql
-- 9) Attach human‑readable director names to those IDs
WITH top_gen AS (
    SELECT g."genre"
    FROM "genre" g
    JOIN "ratings" r ON r."movie_id" = g."movie_id"
    WHERE r."avg_rating" > 8
    GROUP BY g."genre"
    ORDER BY COUNT(DISTINCT g."movie_id") DESC
    LIMIT 3
), top_movies AS (
    SELECT DISTINCT g."movie_id"
    FROM "genre" g
    JOIN "ratings" r ON r."movie_id" = g."movie_id"
    JOIN top_gen tg ON tg."genre" = g."genre"
    WHERE r."avg_rating" > 8
), dir_counts AS (
    SELECT dm."name_id",
